00:06:11.038 --> 00:06:14.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:20.374 --> 00:06:21.790
ALEX WALDROP: Thank
you, Jonathan,

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.910
for that introduction.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:27.880
Today, I want to talk to you
all a little bit about what

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:30.580
our lab is doing in scaling
up to the challenge of cancer

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.640
genomics with Google Cloud
and a software package

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.066
that we've created
called CloudConductor.

00:06:36.066 --> 00:06:38.440
I'm going to tell you something
about the state of cancer

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.290
research that you may not know.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.890
Although we've made tremendous
progress in the past 50 years

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:48.640
on understanding how cancers
arise and how to treat them,

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.830
we've only scratched the
surface in understanding

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:53.230
the true diversity
of the known cancer

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:54.970
types that are in existence.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:59.200
So for instance, the
World Health Organization

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.220
identified--

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:03.880
or currently recognizes
over 1,000 cancer types.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.560
We have good data on
maybe 200 of those.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.640
So the next decades, we'll
be filling in those knowledge

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:10.912
gaps.

00:07:10.912 --> 00:07:12.620
And part of that is
with cancer genomics,

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:14.710
and the state of cancer
genomics actually

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.580
lags even further behind.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:18.730
So take for example the
Cancer Genome Atlas,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.360
TCGA, which Jonathan
just mentioned.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.940
But in that original study,
it took them eight years.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.240
They sequenced 10,000
tumors but just

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.820
from 13 different cancer types.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:34.810
So we have good sequence
data and genomic data

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:37.105
on less than 1% of
the known cancers.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.850
Our lab's goal is simple, and
that is to systematically study

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.330
the genomics of all cancers.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.620
Now before you laugh so
hard that you fall out

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:53.890
of your chair, I want
to say a claim that's

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.100
going to be even more
audacious, and that is our goal

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.820
is to do that within
the next five years.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.120
Now, we're making
progress towards that.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.110
So this past year
in a pilot study

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:09.980
that we did published in "Cell,"
we looked at 1,000 tumors

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:13.820
from a single lymphoma
subtype called DLBCL.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.790
But eventually, that
goal is to scale up

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:20.600
to 100,000 tumors sequenced
from each of those 1,000

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.596
cancers represented.

00:08:22.596 --> 00:08:24.650
How are we making
strides on that?

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:26.290
Well, first off we
did the hard work,

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.420
and we founded a
worldwide consortium

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.370
of over 30 leading
academic hospitals

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.350
from around the world.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.340
So these are helping us
gather patients, enroll them

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.020
in the studies so
that we can have

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:41.580
the diversity of cancer types
represented in that data set.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.039
We've also created a
collaborative Cloud-based

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:49.810
system for collating, collecting
that metadata and those tumors.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.720
So we can store things
like pathology data

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.190
and pathology reviews.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:54.810
Eventually, we'll
be able to link up

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.030
that metadata with
the genomic data

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.820
and understand how patient
outcomes are being influenced

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.490
by patient genotypes.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:08.370
But the more tangible goal that
we'll be reaching this year

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.830
is sequencing
10,000 blood cancers

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:14.580
from over 100 different
lymphoma subtypes.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.450
Now, why blood cancers?

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.180
For one, they're the number one
in terms of cancer diagnosis

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:22.760
and treatment across cancers.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:26.670
For two, with over 100 types,
they're extremely diverse.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.600
And basically, we'll be
knocking out about 10%

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.620
of our goal of all cancers
by just sequencing this one

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:34.920
category.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.490
So we've already enrolled
more than 10,000 patients

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.270
in the study and have collected
more than 10,000 tumor samples.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:44.690
And we've already begun exome
and transcriptome sequencing

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.340
on this project.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:49.310
But a bigger
question is, how can

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:51.140
a group of four
bioinformaticians

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.960
do in four months
what it took TCGA

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.570
and their hundreds of
bioinformaticians almost eight

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:59.210
years to do, and that
is to sequence 10,000

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:02.120
genomes at the same time?

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.520
And maybe to understand
how we're saving time,

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.580
it would be a better
lesson to talk

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.400
about why it is so
time-consuming to sequence

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.880
a genome in order to make
those kind of breakthroughs.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:16.766
So it would be really great
if a DNA sequencer was

00:10:16.766 --> 00:10:18.140
a machine that
picked up a genome

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.930
and let you read it
end-to-end, like a book.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.490
That actually doesn't exist.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:25.190
In practice, a DNA sequencer
is a little bit more

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.980
like a shredder and that
you put the book into,

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:29.660
and it rips it into
a billion pieces

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:33.570
that you get back and
have to assemble later.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:37.700
So in order to read
that story from that bag

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.570
of a billion pieces--

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.460
and by reading this
story in our case,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:46.700
it is identifying a variant
that may be underlying a cancer.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.990
But in any case, the
process of assembling

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:53.450
a bag of a billion
slices of paper

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:56.720
back into an original book
or reassembling a genome

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.780
is very computationally
expensive

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.040
and operationally complex.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.970
It usually involves 15
or 20 third-party tools

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:08.210
being strung together in what
we call a biological pipeline.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:10.430
And this is basically
where you're

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:12.200
piping the output
from one program

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.000
into the output of another.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.530
They have different versions.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:18.230
Each of them has their
own runtime environment.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.990
It's a real pain in the butt to
try to get these things going.

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:24.110
And so from a
computing perspective,

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:26.210
the ad hoc approaches
that people generally use

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:27.920
are not reproducible.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.360
They are time-consuming.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:31.040
And they're very error-prone.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:36.260
And so that's why we are using
and created CloudConductor

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.920
to help solve those
challenges on our way

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.230
to the 10,000 and even
100,000 genomes goal.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:44.730
And to talk about
how CloudConductor

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:48.740
is doing that, I'm going to turn
it over to my colleague, Razvan

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:49.410
Panea, here.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.010
RAZVAN PANEA: Thank you, Alex.

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:04.340
CloudConductor is a Cloud-based
bioinformatics workflow

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.040
management system.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.010
We developed
CloudConductor to address

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:13.350
the four central
challenges in biocomputing.

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:14.760
The first challenge
for computing

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:19.860
is diversity, as many
analysis pipelines in research

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.990
use different bioinformatics
tools in different orders.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.770
So we implemented a system
that allows the researcher

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.860
to run any pipeline.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:31.220
Secondly, scalability is a
problem in bioinformatics,

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.660
as the input sample size
is continuously increasing,

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:38.960
and the institutional computing
resources are limited.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:40.910
Fortunately, cloud
computing platforms,

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.030
such as Google
Cloud, are actually

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:45.360
a solution to this problem.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.910
Furthermore, the total cost of
processing is also a problem.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:51.530
However, we managed
to fix this issue

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.600
by using preemtable
instances from a cloud.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.510
And finally,
portability is actually

00:12:56.510 --> 00:13:00.110
a very serious problem,
as data analysis

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:01.970
needs to be reproducible.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.400
However, we're using
Docker technology

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:07.080
to containerize the
tools in the pipelines.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:10.520
So the researcher can run
the same pipeline anywhere

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:15.180
without having to worry about
affecting the final output.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:18.450
After solving these
challenges, CloudConductor

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:22.110
has become a comprehensive
workflow management system

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:24.520
that works in three big phases.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:27.810
The first phase is represented
by the researcher selecting

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.390
from a list of predefined and
user-defined bioinformatics

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:34.200
tools to generate an analysis
workflow after which,

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.690
CloudConductor conductor
validates, or interprets,

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.160
this workflow.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.490
In the next phase,
CloudConductor

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:46.560
obtains these tools and runs
them in the correct order

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:49.080
by allocating the
necessary resources

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.020
on a cloud-computing platform,
such as Google Cloud,

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:55.230
and by generating a processing
environment using the Docker

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:56.980
system.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.430
After the analysis is
complete, CloudConductor

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.830
could transfer the
[INAUDIBLE] statistics

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:05.620
to a database and
the final output

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:08.290
to an actual cloud
storage system,

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:11.100
such as Google Cloud Storage.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:15.690
Now, in order for our lab
to use the CloudConductor,

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:19.800
we implement an additional
infrastructure layer that fully

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:22.930
automates this entire system.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:26.440
The system starts from
the moment the sequencing

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:30.100
data enters our lab.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.900
At the click of
one single button,

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:35.410
we update the database,
our lab database,

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.920
with the new
sequencing information.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:39.910
And almost immediately
after that,

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:41.620
CloudConductor
daemon, as service

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:45.940
that we implemented, and
continuously waits and watches

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:48.280
the database identifies
the new additions

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.710
and generates a new
instance of CloudConductor

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:52.490
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.330
And after a short
period of time,

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:57.990
after the analysis is
complete, CloudConductor

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:02.070
reports the status of the
analysis to the lab database,

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:05.310
and the final output files
that we'll look later

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:08.480
are transferred to
Google Cloud storage.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:11.270
And as you can observe,
overall, the system

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:16.640
is not only fully-automated,
but it can also be reproducible.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:20.510
And as a proof of concept,
we used an early version

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:24.530
of this pipeline of
the system to process

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:27.160
a large cohort of
1,000 lymphoma patients

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:28.730
that was published in "Cell."

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:30.200
And in the future,
we're planning

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:35.060
to use this system to increase
the sample size not only by 10

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.849
times but even 100 times.

00:15:37.849 --> 00:15:39.640
Before I close, I would
like to acknowledge

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:44.590
our lab, [INAUDIBLE] Dave, a
co-developer of CloudConductor.

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:47.290
And in conclusion,
CloudConductor

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:51.160
is helping our lab
create the largest cancer

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:53.900
datasets in the world.

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:57.290
Using the right
tools, cloud computing

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:02.640
helps small labs, like ours,
do data analysis at the highest

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:04.580
institutional skill level.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:06.690
And using all these
systems, we're

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:10.260
able to get faster
breakthroughs,

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:14.230
develop better treatments, and
get closer and closer to curing

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.110
the disease that we study.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:17.270
We'll take questions.

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:18.230
Thank you.

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:21.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.060
And now, I'd like to
introduce Jonathan LeFaive

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.875
from University of Michigan.

00:16:29.875 --> 00:16:33.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:45.977 --> 00:16:48.310
JONATHAN LEFAIVE: Thank you,
and good morning, everyone.

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:55.630
I suspect most of you have heard
of the term precision medicine.

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:59.440
The idea is to provide medical
treatments and screenings that

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.654
are tailored to an individual's
unique characteristics.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:11.940
Take high cholesterol,
for example--

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.760
LDL cholesterol, which is often
referred to as bad cholesterol,

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.790
can build up in your arteries,
resulting in heart disease.

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.190
The usual cause of this is
unhealthy lifestyle choices

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.810
pertaining to diet and exercise.

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:25.640
But sometimes, this
can be inherited.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:30.180
One gene responsible for
this is called PCSK9.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:33.870
This gene produces a troublesome
enzyme of the same name.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:37.050
This enzyme binds to naturally
occurring LDL receptors,

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:41.070
preventing these receptors from
removing the bad cholesterol.

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:45.100
After discovering this, PCSK9
inhibitors were developed.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.610
These are drugs that
neutralized the enzymes'

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:50.490
effect on cholesterol levels.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.740
But the treatment won't be
equally effective for everyone.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:56.920
Rare mutations in this gene
can either make it overactive

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:59.710
or knock out its
activity altogether.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:03.400
But by looking at a patient's
DNA, it's possible for doctors

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.860
to determine whether or
not these inhibitors would

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:09.830
be the most effective
treatment for their patient.

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:12.550
This is an example of a genetic
defect that we do know about,

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:14.500
but there's still
plenty to be learned

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:18.820
before precision medicine
can reach its full potential.

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:20.730
The National Heart,
Lung, and Blood Institute

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.730
is sponsoring a project
called TOPMed that is focused

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.980
on closing this knowledge gap.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.940
But for projects like
this to study DNA,

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.050
they must first collect DNA.

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:33.990
A traditional method
for this is using

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.990
what is called a microarray.

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:39.530
It targets small
subsets of the genome,

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:42.380
supplying researchers with
tens to hundreds of thousands

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.440
of genetic markers to analyze.

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.820
This technique is
relatively inexpensive,

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:50.660
which makes it attractive
for use in both research

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:54.590
and consumer services, like
23andMe and ancestry.com.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:58.710
And while this method is useful
for targeting common variations

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.710
of DNA, it lacks
the scope needed

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:06.200
to find rare associations
like those in PCSK9.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.070
For this, we need to
use a method called

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:15.900
whole genome sequencing, which
looks at the entire genome.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:17.970
With this process, a
lab physically sequences

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.280
short, accurate reads of DNA.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:21.870
These reads are
then algorithmically

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:25.320
aligned, or mapped,
to a reference genome

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.347
and include a lot of
overlap, or depth,

00:19:28.347 --> 00:19:30.180
to ensure that what
we're seeing in the data

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.640
isn't due to random error.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.710
The TOPMed initiative is
employing this technique

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:39.150
with 130,000 individuals and
has produced data sets up

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:42.330
to 600 million variants--

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:44.880
in other words, 600
million potential avenues

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.870
for discovery.

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:51.530
40% of these are extremely
rare and have likely never

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:53.660
been seen before.

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:55.460
Only with this level
of information can

00:19:55.460 --> 00:20:00.350
we study how rare mutations
are associated with disease.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.879
Now, at this point, some of
you may be thinking, well,

00:20:02.879 --> 00:20:03.420
that's great.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.810
But what does this have
to do with Google Cloud?

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:07.360
So going forward,
I'm going to talk

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:11.240
about the technical challenges
of whole genome sequencing,

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:14.120
how and why we leverage Google
Cloud Platform to surmount

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:17.120
these challenges, and how the
Cloud is helping us foster

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:20.390
collaboration in the research.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:22.910
So here's how whole genome
sequencing breaks down in terms

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:24.870
of data size and compute.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.020
At the scale of
TOPMed, it produces

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:30.410
approximately three petabytes
of highly-compressed sequence

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:31.610
data.

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:36.200
And mapping these reads
takes around 6,000 core years

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:37.820
to compute.

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:40.052
That is a long time to
wait, and we haven't even

00:20:40.052 --> 00:20:41.010
gotten the science yet.

00:20:43.456 --> 00:20:44.830
At the University
of Michigan, we

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:48.990
are warehousing, mapping, and
generating aggregate datasets

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:50.760
of TOPMed data so that
the genomes can be

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:53.850
used in association analyzes.

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:57.300
Using our local cluster which
has a few thousand cores,

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:01.060
it would take years to tackle
this amount of sequence data.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.210
But with the seemingly limitless
resources on Google Compute

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:07.440
Engine, we can compress a
couple of years of data prep

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.900
into a few months.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.750
This means researchers can
start analyzing the data

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:14.310
and making discoveries
much sooner.

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:18.930
This convenience
does come at a cost,

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:21.630
and researchers tend to
have limited budgets.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.310
But there are a
couple of features

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:25.680
which go beyond simply having
competitive prices that

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:30.690
make Google Cloud Platform
stand out in this regard.

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:33.210
Unlike other cloud providers
that restrict users

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:35.490
to predefined machine
types, Compute Engine

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:38.820
is completely modular,
allowing resource allocations

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:42.950
that meet the exact
needs of a given task.

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:45.440
These fine-tuned controls
allow those of us

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:49.061
with limited budgets to
maximize every penny.

00:21:49.061 --> 00:21:51.190
The preemptal machines can
take those cost savings

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:52.990
even further.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:55.810
As many of you already, GCP
offers their idle resource

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.690
reserves at a fraction of
the costs but with the caveat

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:03.190
that they can be preempted
or taken away at any moment.

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:05.500
In order to reduce the
time lost from preemption,

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.990
we have altered our pipelines
to be more resilient.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:11.680
We've done this by chunking
our compute jobs into smaller

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:13.690
pieces and
checkpointing the output

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:16.750
files into Cloud storage.

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:19.210
With this approach, we can
still make progress, even

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:20.170
when preemption occurs.

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:25.830
Cloud storage is also
serving another purpose.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:28.270
TOPMed is a collaboration
of approximately 30 studies

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:30.430
across many institutions.

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.620
Each study has focused
expertise in diseases

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:37.660
such as asthma, sickle cell
disease, atrial fibrillation,

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:39.660
and heart disease.

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.040
Making these
discoveries requires

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:43.590
a diverse knowledge
base, so genomic datasets

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:46.380
like these have traditionally
been distributed to the experts

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.360
to be analyzed in
our local clusters.

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:52.510
But this is becoming
more and more

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:55.090
difficult to handle as
the data sizes increase,

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:58.502
especially for smaller labs.

00:22:58.502 --> 00:22:59.960
The National
Institute of Health is

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:03.320
piloting an ecosystem
using TOPMed data following

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.320
a different approach.

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:08.030
Instead of sending
data to the scientist,

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:11.890
we are bringing the
scientists to the data.

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:13.350
It's called the
NIH Data Commons,

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:16.230
and it will empower researchers
from institutions of all sizes

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:18.459
to form high-throughput
computations

00:23:18.459 --> 00:23:19.500
within Google's platform.

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:26.980
But even within this space, data
access needs to be regulated.

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:28.630
When DNA is donated,
it is often given

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:30.490
with specific consent
that determine

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:33.700
what it can be used for, and
these consents vary widely

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:36.250
from study to study.

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:39.220
The Data Commons is building
frameworks for access control

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:42.940
on top of the existing
cloud storage APIs,

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.280
and we're doing this to
ensure that these consents are

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:47.440
respected.

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.740
These frameworks range from
programmatically generating

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:53.430
signed URLs to fuse
file system limitations

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:55.730
that turn buckets
into a mounted disk.

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:03.770
Google Cloud's Container
Support is another great feature

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:05.570
for this type of environment.

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:08.450
Scientists love containers,
and it's not just

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:11.700
because it's easy to deploy
complicated pipelines

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:14.450
but because it makes
it easier for peers

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:16.550
to reproduce your work.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:22.147
And reproducibility is
critical in science.

00:24:22.147 --> 00:24:23.730
Google's private
container [? repos ?]

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:25.830
are backed by Cloud Storage.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:28.560
This allows for bloated
container images

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:30.360
to be pooled more quickly.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:33.150
And just like typical
objects in Cloud Storage,

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:36.060
access controls can be applied,
allowing you to safely share

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:42.650
images that contain sensitive
health or genetic information.

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:45.250
So by leveraging the
Cloud in these ways,

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:48.915
we can scale to unprecedented
levels of data storage

00:24:48.915 --> 00:24:53.026
and compute, allowing us to and
make discoveries more quickly.

00:24:53.026 --> 00:24:54.400
We can bring
researchers together

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:57.190
to work on a level playing field
and in a highly-collaborative

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:58.730
environment.

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:02.438
And in doing so, we can quicken
the pace of medical progress.

00:25:02.438 --> 00:25:03.394
Thank you.

00:25:03.394 --> 00:25:07.218
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:10.570 --> 00:25:13.270
Up next, we have the
Director of Bioinformatics

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:17.830
at Stanford University,
Sowmi Utiramerur.

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:19.540
SOWMI UTIRAMERUR:
Thanks, Jonathan.

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:20.510
Morning, everyone.

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:21.310
I'm Sowmi.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:23.350
As any true academic
will do, I've

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:27.040
collected multiple titles which
I will not bore you with today.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.340
But in essence, my role
is to drive adoption

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:32.170
of genomics in medicine.

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:36.350
Jonathan already mentioned
about precision medicine.

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:37.807
So what does
precision medicine--

00:25:37.807 --> 00:25:39.640
since I made pretty
slides with light bulbs,

00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:41.014
I'm going to
reiterate the point.

00:25:41.014 --> 00:25:42.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:47.350
We take genomic sequencing
data of the patient.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:51.580
We combine with sequencing
data from other patients

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:53.170
with similar disease
or disorders,

00:25:53.170 --> 00:25:55.330
even healthy individuals,
for that matter.

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:58.840
We analyze them together with
all available annotations,

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:00.760
or publicly-available
annotations,

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:05.210
to come up with a precise
diagnosis for that patient.

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:08.620
And finally, we tailor the
treatment not just based

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:12.040
on the disease but to the
particular genetic makeup

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:14.450
of the patient
that is a concern.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:18.140
So in essence, we are trying
to be Starbucks for medicine.

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.680
So at Stanford, our
mission is to deliver

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:24.890
on the promise of precision
medicine for all our patients.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:28.150
And we do so by using
genomic sequencing.

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:31.960
Primarily, we are focused
on childhood rare diseases,

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:37.070
inherited cancer, cardio,
and neurological disorders.

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.120
So that's the promise of
precision medicine, but what

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:41.500
are the challenges?

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:43.960
So there are several challenges,
principal among which

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:45.400
is that we have
yet to understand

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:47.920
the regulatory mechanisms
of many of the genes

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:50.290
and how their functions
correlate at a more-- the

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:51.610
pathways level.

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.530
But there are more
mundane or fundamental

00:26:53.530 --> 00:26:55.420
challenges which we
are yet to overcome,

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:58.640
and principal among which,
as Jonathan mentioned,

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:01.320
repeatability and
reproducibility,

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.790
which is paramount when
you're using anything

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:05.920
in a diagnostic setting.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:08.530
So if you're a diabetic,
you're taking a blood test

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:09.910
to measure your sugar levels.

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:13.210
If the instrument gives two
widely different measurements

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:16.570
at different times
it makes measurement,

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.220
you're not going
to rely on that.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:20.680
Genetic tests are
extremely complex,

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:23.530
primarily because we use
multiple instruments, hundreds

00:27:23.530 --> 00:27:25.120
of different analytical
methods which

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:27.220
runs on different hardware
platforms, whether it

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:30.490
be laptop, in-house
cluster, or on the Cloud.

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:33.790
But yet, we are expected to
reproduce the same result not

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:38.720
just on a given day but up
to two years into the future.

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:41.650
And the second biggest
point, as-- that everybody

00:27:41.650 --> 00:27:44.320
and the other panelists
mentioned-- is security.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:46.570
Anybody who has done
anything on the Cloud

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.330
is primarily worried
about data security.

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:52.660
And this is extremely important
when you put patient health

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:54.190
information on the Cloud.

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:57.190
This essentially gives
people fits and makes

00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:59.410
us lose sleep every night.

00:27:59.410 --> 00:28:02.140
But how do we overcome
these challenges?

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:05.320
Primarily with the help of
container-optimized engine

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:07.480
virtual machines from Google.

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:10.300
And HIPAA compatibility
and certification

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:14.770
of all GCP components helps
us achieve this goal much more

00:28:14.770 --> 00:28:17.200
easily and removes
the heavy lift

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:20.260
of maintaining this hardware
from small clinical labs,

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:24.890
like us, which essentially,
we are not experts in.

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:29.050
So here is the example of how
we have solved it in Stanford.

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:31.990
You already heard about
Docker, which is essentially

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:34.340
crucial for reproducibility.

00:28:34.340 --> 00:28:36.430
We have our own
workflow manager.

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:38.530
Of course, we have
to have our own.

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:41.060
We cannot just use others'.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:46.450
So ours is called LOOM,
which essentially understands

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.120
workflow language, pulls
in the Docker container

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:51.430
that you need for
execution step,

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:53.500
imports all the
files that is needed,

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:56.530
hashes them to make sure that
the files have not changed.

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:59.590
If so, it warns that
the workflow is not

00:28:59.590 --> 00:29:02.180
reproducible at the
end of the execution,

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:04.900
shuts down the VM, and
copies all the files back

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:06.760
to the specified storage.

00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:09.700
This way, we can ship off the
container and the workflow

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:13.700
language to any institution, and
they can reproduce the results.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:15.460
But let's get to
a bigger problem.

00:29:15.460 --> 00:29:18.745
And the bigger problem
is that any genetic test,

00:29:18.745 --> 00:29:21.820
the average diagnostic
yield is around 30%.

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:24.720
Of course, for certain diseases,
it's much higher than 30%,

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:26.650
and for certain, it's lower.

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:29.550
But what do we mean by that?

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:32.890
For all the patients that walk
in with a genetic disorder

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:36.930
into our lab, we are able to
give a precise diagnosis only,

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:40.690
for on average, 30%
of the cases, which

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:42.890
we have to improve upon.

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:45.970
So I'll give you two specific
patient study examples

00:29:45.970 --> 00:29:48.220
in the next couple of
slides to highlight

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:50.590
the importance of
adopting novel sequencing

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:54.910
methods, analytical methods,
and data mining and data sharing

00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:59.140
in order to be able to improve
upon the set diagnostic yield.

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:02.350
So the first example
is a patient case

00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:06.520
with Carney complex with cardiac
myxoma, which is-- essentially,

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:10.100
Carney complex leads to benign
tumors in different issue

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:11.630
types, primarily skin.

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:14.210
But when it gets
too hard, it leads

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:17.720
to weakening of our valves
and ultimately requiring

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:19.140
a heart transplant.

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:20.840
So this is a patient case.

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:24.710
We had a suspicion that--
this is a genetic disorder.

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.690
We know the gene
that causes this.

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:27.560
But we sequenced it.

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:29.450
We could not find
the change that

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:31.440
was causal in this patient.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:34.820
So in addition to giving them
a short-read sequencing, which

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:36.380
we typically do
for all patients,

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:39.290
we used a new technology
called long-read sequencing

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:42.230
from PacBio, which
led us to identify

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:44.420
a long deletion,
two-exon deletion,

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:46.714
in this gene, which
is deleted in one

00:30:46.714 --> 00:30:48.380
copy of the gene
versus the other, which

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:50.480
makes it much more
harder to find

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.160
with short-read sequencing.

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:54.770
And we had to develop normal
sequencing methods in order

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:56.270
to be able to analyze this data.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:58.520
And this is where
the contribution

00:30:58.520 --> 00:31:00.860
of Google AI and
Google Genomics,

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:03.320
like Deep Gradient
from [INAUDIBLE],,

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.440
is very critical for us
to be able to advance

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:08.810
this process of being
able to identify

00:31:08.810 --> 00:31:10.700
novel genetic gradients,
which we were not

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:14.160
able to do otherwise.

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:16.710
And second example
highlights the importance

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:19.590
of data sharing and data mining.

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:22.110
This is a case of
two siblings affected

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:26.490
with several neurological
phenotypes, primarily delayed

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:29.700
intellectual development,
cerebellar ataxia, which is,

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:32.610
essentially, you lose
control of your muscles,

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:35.020
atrophy of the brain itself.

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:39.120
So we sequenced these patients.

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:42.890
In this case, we found a
mutation in the SNX14 gene,

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:46.440
but we just involved our--
implicated in brain development

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:47.280
in mouse models.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:49.307
But we're not seeing this
in any other patients,

00:31:49.307 --> 00:31:51.390
so we weren't sure whether
this was the real cause

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:52.960
of the mutation or not.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:56.796
But purely by chance, we
identified another patient

00:31:56.796 --> 00:31:58.170
in the sidelines
of a conference,

00:31:58.170 --> 00:32:01.590
like this, that another patient
who had the same symptoms

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:03.930
and the same mutation
in that gene, which

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:06.990
led us to conclude that this
is the true causal gradient

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:09.360
in this gene for
this patient, which

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:12.450
ended a 10-year diagnostic
odyssey for these patients.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:15.340
In most of these cases,
these are not curable,

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:17.730
but just finding the
cause of a disease

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:20.450
is so much help
for these patients,

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:22.500
that there's a hope,
at least, that there is

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:24.750
a cure for them in the future.

00:32:24.750 --> 00:32:27.600
So the importance of this
data binding or data sharing

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:30.960
is really important, because
these are rare diseases where

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.960
only a handful of people
and handful of mutations

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:35.880
are found in genes like this.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:37.830
So finally, I would
like to conclude

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:42.420
by raising the
importance of platforms

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:45.160
like Google Cloud and other--

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:48.530
I think, even though it's
not in their core business,

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:50.310
the contributions
made by Google AI

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:55.230
and Google Genomics helps drive
genomics research much forward

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:58.620
and being able to adapt this in
precision medicine a whole lot

00:32:58.620 --> 00:32:59.520
faster.

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:03.570
With that, I'd like to thank
everyone and invite Jonathan

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:04.840
back for Q&amp;A.

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:08.221
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:11.119 --> 00:33:13.550
JONATHAN SHEFFI: Thanks so much.

00:33:13.550 --> 00:33:14.750
All right, thank you all.

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:18.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

